## Orient and assess

- Read `agent.md`, `HABITS.md`, wiki home and relevant topic maps under the memory root. Follow relative links from the current page's directory, not the workspace root.
- Inventory `memories/*.md` and `memories/projects/<project-key>/*.md`. Domain maps and INDEX are navigation, not factual sources. Do not create legacy USER.md or MEMORY.md.
- Use `memory_search` to find overlapping notes, then read their full source before consolidating. Do not infer a duplicate from filenames, snippets or truncation alone. Avoid rereading the entire corpus when only a few topics changed.
- Check instructions against explicit user corrections; verify technical claims against current source. A newer timestamp alone does not prove a claim true. Distinguish project scope, current reference, dated observations and unresolved work.

## Consolidate without losing meaning

- Update existing canonical topic pages under `{{ memory_dir }}memories/`, not arbitrary files at the agent root. Keep each project's lessons scoped to that project; do not merge similar statements from different repositories as universal facts.
- Preserve every unique constraint, decision, correction, provenance, verification result and unresolved issue. Consolidate repeated explanations into concise rules. Keep detailed incident history separately with a link from the current page.
- Read before editing. Use `edit_file` for existing files and `write_file` only for new ones. Never overwrite a memory with a small note or treat it as scratch space. Preserve the exact originals in a backup before each additional rewrite.
- Keep explicit user preferences intact. Resolve a conflict only from applicable user corrections and verified evidence; ask if an ambiguity changes the required behavior. Never import another user's preferences or infer consent from generated memory.
- Keep agent.md for stable preferences and HABITS.md for a stable checklist. Put new incident reviews in topic memory, not above the checklist. Essential rules go first; the default block injection cap is 6,000 characters (smaller windows/configuration can reduce it). Aim for a checklist under 2,000 characters. Do not raise context limits to accommodate growth.
- When creating or consolidating a stable HABITS.md checklist, retain original review history in a linked topic or backup and include `<!-- nova-memory-wiki:checklist -->` under its title. This tells background reviews to save new incident lessons in habits-lessons.md instead of displacing the checklist. Use named Work Checklist, Final Review and Completion Report sections; remove references to sections that do not exist.
- Add concise related-page links and a topic-map/home route. Keep source claims ahead of navigation so retrieval snippets retain facts.
- Before retiring a duplicate page, preserve its full original, compare complete sources, move all unique facts, and repair inbound links. Prefer a redirect page over deletion. Do not delete source files, histories or backups as a shortcut to savings. If unsure, retain both and record the conflict.
- If files changed concurrently, re-read and reconcile; do not overwrite the newer version. If interrupted, leave the backups and report remaining work so the next pass can continue.

## Rebuild and verify

- Discover `memory_wiki` with `search_tools` if needed. Call `memory_wiki(action="maintain")` after edits to rebuild the home index, topic maps and related links from actual files. It preserves page bodies; you remain responsible for semantic consolidation and repairing reported unresolved links.
- Keep INDEX below 2,000 characters and {{ index_max_lines | default(100) }} lines. It should route to maps, not duplicate every topic. Maps index all current topic pages. Never remove a unique page merely to satisfy an index budget.
- Call `memory_wiki(action="check")` and read its result. Check root-rule consistency, named checklist sections, complete topic reachability and current project scope. Repair genuine broken wiki links; historical source-code references may need an explicit historical label rather than a fabricated target.
- If the maintenance tool is unavailable, edit the same wiki structure through file tools and explicitly validate every added local link and orphan page; report that automatic validation was unavailable.

## Report

State what was consolidated, corrected and linked, where originals were backed up, and any unresolved conflicts, broken links or incomplete work. Distinguish structural checks from actual model recall quality. If no semantic change was justified, say so. Never claim consolidation merely because an index was rebuilt.
